-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Normalization:** Normalize the input volume `vol` to the 8-bit integer range $[0, 255]$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Threshold Ordering:** The transfer function logic assumes $l_1 < l_2 < l_3 < l_4$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Shape Clarification:** The source code initializes `ps` as `np.zeros([dim[1], dim[2], dim[3]])` [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Alpha Inversion:** The final alpha channel is inverted ($1 - A$) [S1, unofficial].
-*   This is non-standard for direct volume rendering, which typically outputs accumulated opacity [G].
-*   **Slice-Based Compositing:** The method composites strictly along the volume's depth axis (slices), not arbitrary rays [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** $O(D \cdot H \cdot W)$ for transfer function application and $O(D \cdot H \cdot W)$ for compositing, where $D, H, W$ are volume dimensions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(D \cdot H \cdot W)$ for the intermediate `trans_vol` array [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method is CPU-based and implemented in Python using NumPy [S1, unofficial]. It is not optimized for real-time interaction due to the two-pass nature and Python overhead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, easy to understand, interactive transfer function control via GUI [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High memory usage due to intermediate RGBA volume, slow performance on large volumes, non-standard alpha inversion [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(D \cdot H \cdot W)$ for the intermediate 4D RGBA volume `fres` [S1, unofficial]. This is higher than single-pass ray casting which does not store the full intermediate volume [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, interactive control via piecewise linear transfer function [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory usage due to intermediate volume storage; two-pass approach is less efficient than single-pass ray casting; alpha inversion in final output is non-standard [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Educational purposes or simple interactive demos where memory is not a constraint [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Large volumes or production rendering where memory efficiency and performance are critical [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from a GitHub repository by Dakai Zhou, titled "Volume-Rendering" [S1, unofficial]. It is described as a demo of user-interactive volume rendering using ray casting in Python, implemented from scratch without image processing packages [S1, unofficial]. The code was tested on MR images, though CT images are recommended for better results [S1, unofficial]. The method uses a piecewise linear transfer function with 5 control points and front-to-back compositing [S1, unofficial]. A parallel implementation using MPI and binary-swap compositing is also described in a separate repository by rishit-AI, which uses similar transfer function interpolation and compositing formulas [S2, unofficial].
+Originated from a Python-based educational demo by Dakai Zhou, aiming to understand the ray casting algorithm from scratch without image processing packages [S1, unofficial]. It uses DICOM images as input, though CT images are recommended over MR images for better volume rendering results [S1, unofficial]. The method is implemented in `DirectVolRendering.py` and `InteractiveDirectVolRendering.py` [S1, unofficial].
 
